“Friendly Joan Ryan has received such a treatment because he redetermibbed anti-Se anti-Se anti-Se anti-Se anti-Se anti-Se anti-Semit.
“In my case, the hurdle motion criticised me to express my disagreement with Jeremy Corbyn.
On the importance of a responsible economic policy, on national security, on Europe, ironically all topics similar to those on which Jeremy disagreed with previous leaders.
“We want the meetings at Nottingham East Labour on a Friday’s notice that “receied for meetings to be inclusive and productive.”
For the majority of my eight-Yeenth as a Member of the Parliament of the Local Labour Party, Friday night meetings were exactly like that.
However, this is not the tone of numerous gatherings and the promise of a “kinder and courteer” policy has long been forgotten if it has ever been remembered.
“It’s become increasingly obvious that different viewpoints aren’t tolerated in the Labour Party, and every opinion is judged on whether or not it’s acceptable to party leadership.
All this began shortly after Jeremy became the head of the party, and colleagues with whom before I thought I was sharing a similar political vision they began to expect a reversal of the trend on my part and to take positions that I would never accept, whether it was national security or the European single market.
“When I speak in public – and it doesn’t really matter what I’m saying—we follow invectives of insults on social media demanding my retirement, denouncing the center’s policy and telling me that I shouldn’t be a part of the Labour Party.
It’s not just my experience.
“I certainly know that I’m luckier than my other co-workers because the comments directed to me tend to be political in nature.
"I am admired by the professionalism and determination of those colleagues who have to face a creek of insults with a sessist or racist background every day but who do not get intimidated.
“One of the most disappointing aspects of this political age is the normalization of insult levels.
“Jurie Corbyn said last week that the Labour Party should promote a culture of tolerance.
“The reality is that we’re no longer in a tolerant environment and with every no-confidential dormous or modification of selection rules, the party shrinks.
Over the last couple of years I’ve been given a lot of advice, urging me to lower my heads so openly and everything “would go well”.
But that’s not to do what I went into politics.
“Since I joined the Labour Party 32 years ago as an anointer, wrought by the indignant of the Thatcher government who had left my secondary school class literally falling apart, I have tried to defend better public services for those most in need, as local councillor or government minister.
“I never hid my political opinions, not even at the last election.
“No one at Nottingham East could have in any way bewildered by my political stances and points of disagreement with the current leadership.
To those who promoted the motion on Friday, I’d like to say that when the country is plunging into a Brexit that will hurt households, companies and our public services, I don’t understand a desire to waste time and energy by talking about my loyalty to the Labour party leader.
“Truly the only message I’ve got isn’t for Nottingham Momentum, but for my constituents, whether they’re a member of the Labour Party or not: I’m proud to serve you and I’m proud that no threat of exclusion or political opportunity will stop me from acting in defense of what I consider to be your best interests.
Chris Leslie is Member of Parliament for Nottingham East
