Harvard Students Take a Course on How to Reslept Enough
“Wrought a new course at Harvard University this year prompted all of its students to sleep more in an attempt to thwart the centuries-a-chief-and-breeding culture of studying for whole-day caffeine-powered nights.
An under-graduate scholar has found that students at the world’s number-to-university often don’t have an unmasculate idea of caring for themselves.
Charles Czeisler, Professor of Sleep Mession at Harvard Medical School and a specialist at the Brigham and Women’s Hospital, has developed the course, which he considers to be the first of its kind in the United States.
He had the idea of starting the course after a conference he had given on the impact of lack of sleep on learning.
“At the end a girl came to me and said, ‘Why am I being told this only now when I’m in the last year?’
“He said that no one had ever told her anything about the importance of sleep, which surprised me very much,” he told The Telegraph.
The course, given for the first time this year, explains to the students the essential concepts of how good sleep-related habits can help academic and athletic performance, as well as improve overall well-being.
“Paul Barreira, a professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and director-general of health services at the university, stated that the university decided to introduce the course after discovering that students suffered from a severe lack of sleep during the week.
The one-and-a-hour course includes a range of interactive activities.
In a section there is the image of a dormitory room, where students can select cups of coffee, curtains, sneakers and books in order to be able to know the effects of caffeine and light and how athletic performance is influenced by the lack of sleep, and the importance of an evening routine.
In another section, attendees are explained how long-running lack of sleep can increase the risks of heart attacks, strokes, depression and cancer.
On-ccampus map, with interactive icons, then encourages participants to think about their daily routine.
“We don’t know it’s going to change student behavior immediately.
