“What’s the Global Citizen Festival and has done something to reduce poverty?
This Saturday New York will host the Global Citizen Festival, an annual musical event that sees the performance of a remarkable roundup of stars and has an equally remarkable mission: to put an end to poverty in the world.
Arriving in its 7th edition, the Global Citizen Festival will see tens of thousands of people flock to the Great Lawn of Central Park not only to enjoy performances such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also an outrequiach about the real purpose of the event to end extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, which began in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Project, an international support group hoping to eradicate poverty by increasing the number of people who actively fight it.
In order to receive a free ticket for the event (unless you’re unwilling to pay for a VIP ticket), the concert audience had to complete a range of homework, or “actions” such as volunteering, sending emails to a world leader, making a phone call or any other significant way that helped raise awareness about their purpose of eradicating poverty.
But, of course, how successful did the Global Citizen Festival have had now that there are 12 years left to achieve the set goal?
The idea of refining people with a free concert is indeed a genuine way of persuading people to call for action, or is it just another example of so-called “clicktivism”—people who really make a difference simply by signing an online petition or sending a tweet?
Since 2011, Global Citizen has registered more than 19 million “shares” of its supporters, working for a bunch of different purposes.
It declares that these actions have contributed to urging world leaders to announce commitments and policies of more than $37 billion aimed at influencing the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
In the early years of 2018, the group cited 390 commitments and announcements resulting from their actions, of which at least $10 billion have already been disbursed or used.
The group estimates that protected capital funds have so far had a direct impact on about 649 million people in the world.
Some of the key engagements include “The Power of Nutrition”, a UK-based investor-executor’s partnership committed to “helping children grow to their full potential”, with the promise of providing Rwanda with $35 million to help eliminate malnutrition in the country after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from Global Citizens.
“Botterened with the support of the U.K. government, donors, national governments, and Global Citizens just like you, we can cause social injustice or malnutrition to become a footnote across the story,” The Power d’Ban of Nutrition Ullman told the crowd at a live concert in London in April of 2018.
The group also said that after more than 5,000 actions were put in place appealing to the U.K. to improve the nutrition of mothers and children, the government has announced the granting of funds for a project, the Power of Nutrition, which will reach 5 million women and children through interventions in favor of nutrition.
Responding to one of the FAQs present on its website, which asked: “Which makes you think that we can put an end to extreme poverty?”
“It’s going to be a long and arduous path – we’re sometimes going to cadage and fail.
“But, like big civil rights and anti-to-orrethigate movements before us, we will succeed, because together we’re stronger.
